{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793654059,"nonce":"Nji98gActeHLw2t8OKepARdVfKs=","message":"Owner presentation and startup requirements, 2026-11-02\n\nBelow each CN/block component description, use exactly these tabs: Preview | Live | Code. Preview is selected initially and shows a high-resolution animated GIF produced from the existing headless recorder. Its purpose is to give an immediate first impression while the live WASM demo loads in a background worker as soon as the visitor opens that component page.\n\nThis replaces the earlier proposal to wait for the Live tab before loading WASM. Preload the current component's demo on page visit, not the entire catalog. GIF preparation and live-demo warm-up run in parallel; loading must not delay the description, page controls or preview.\n\nVisitor flow and readiness\n1. Render description, tabs and GIF/poster immediately. Preview remains the default tab.\n2. Start a dedicated worker to fetch and compile the selected versioned WASM module. Prefetch the matching JS glue and required demo assets through the appropriate host/loader paths. Cache compatible resources by bundle/source fingerprint and avoid duplicate downloads or compilation across tab changes.\n3. Pass the compiled WebAssembly.Module from the worker via structured cloning to the demo iframe. Use the matching wasm-bindgen initializer with the compiled module. Initialize the real demo behind the preview at a valid, nonzero viewport so renderer/font/asset setup and first-frame work happen while the visitor watches the GIF.\n4. Distinguish module-compiled, runtime-initializing and interactive-ready. The Live Ready indicator requires the actual demo's first rendered frame and installed input handlers, not merely a resolved wasm-bindgen init promise: the existing start shim spawns asynchronous WebApp setup.\n5. Selecting Live reveals the already-warmed instance. If it is still initializing, keep the preview and a loading indicator in the Live panel until ready. Preserve the selected tab and focus; readiness alone does not switch tabs automatically.\n6. Code shows the exact demo Rust and CSS. Switching back to Preview/Code suspends live rendering where supported while retaining the ready instance. Navigation disposes the current worker/demo resources; a new page/request ID prevents stale readiness from activating the wrong component.\n7. On load/adapter/asset failure, retain the GIF, documentation and Code with a retry action for Live. Index cards keep bounded static previews; opening a detail page starts its current demo warm-up.\n\nWorker boundary grounded in current code\n- Worker loading means off-main-thread fetch/compilation. The current WebApp::new requires window/document/HtmlCanvasElement, and generated wasm-bindgen initialization invokes the start shim. Instantiating that unchanged application inside a worker would fail; canvas/runtime initialization stays in the iframe's DOM context after worker preparation.\n- Existing generated JS supports initializing from a WebAssembly.Module; verify this with the actual catalog build and browser matrix. Full worker-side rendering via OffscreenCanvas is a separate runner capability, not a prerequisite for background loading. If added later, it needs input/resize/font/platform service support rather than simply importing the existing application into a worker.\n- Add a proper warm-up/first-frame/suspend contract. A CSS-hidden zero-size canvas is not a valid warm-up target, and the current self-perpetuating frame loop should not animate invisibly forever. Give the prewarmed surface its eventual layout size and prevent it receiving keyboard/pointer focus while Preview is selected.\n\nHeadless GIF production\n- Reuse blinc_recorder::Headless, deterministic clock stepping, selector-driven input and CapturedFrame output. FrameSequence currently exports numbered PNG frames; add the GIF encoding/export stage around this capture path instead of building another UI recorder.\n- Each demo's catalog entry identifies its shared Rust/CSS source, scripted interaction scenario, logical viewport, raster scale, theme/fonts, capture cadence, duration, loop/reset and poster frame. The recording, WASM entry and Code snippet use the same demo implementation and versioned assets.\n- Capture real high-resolution frames with logical layout size independent of output pixel density. Audit/extend the headless sizing API for that contract, rather than merely enlarging the layout viewport or upscaling a completed PNG.\n- Explicitly select matching theme/font assets for headless and WASM output. Native host theme defaults must not silently give the GIF a different appearance from Live.\n- Record meaningful interaction sequences such as hover/press, popup open/close or progress, with a clean loop. All visual styling remains CSS-first; scripts supply inputs and application-owned Signal updates. Preserve source frames/posters, stream or bound frame buffering, optimize GIF encoding, and visually check text edges, timing and palette/gradient quality.\n- Generate published preview assets from the catalog recording scenarios; the machine-local, platform-specific .blinc/snapshots goldens are not automatically the website asset source.\n- Preview honors reduced motion with a static poster and supports an explicit play/pause presentation. Reserve the final preview dimensions to avoid layout jumps. GIFs should not continue decoding/animating unnecessarily while their panel is inactive.\n\nAcceptance additions\n- Check the tab order and placement under the description, GIF-first paint, worker start on page visit, and that clicking Live does not initiate a redundant cold load.\n- Measure page first impression and end-to-end interactive readiness separately: download, worker compilation, main-context instantiation, asset/font/GPU setup and first frame. Avoid promising an instant Live switch before those measurements.\n- Cover early Live clicks, switching tabs while loading, stale worker completion after navigation, bootstrap failure/retry, reset, viewport changes and repeated navigation without accumulating contexts/timers.\n- All requirements apply to both CN and blocks. No implementation, recording, builds or source edits were performed for this planning update.\n\nSources inspected: blinc_recorder/src/testing/{headless,framebuffer}.rs; blinc_app/src/web.rs; tools/build-web-examples/src/main.rs; existing wasm-bindgen generated web loaders.\nBrowser references: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/WebAssembly/Reference/JavaScript_interface/Module and https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/WebAssembly/Reference/JavaScript_interface/compileStreaming_static.","files":null}]}